Inside the cluster, everything is yours. The edge is where that stops: a cloud
load balancer you did not write, a proxy shared by every tenant, a certificate
authority with an opinion, and — if you take the offer — a CDN that terminates
TLS before you ever see the request. This is the edge half of [the k3s starter
kit](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/).

Three questions come up every time. Do websockets work through a Gateway API
ingress (yes). Where did the client's IP address go (the load balancer ate it).
Should there be a WAF or a CDN in front of any of it (probably not).

::: note
**Part 3 of 7 of [Where to start with self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/)**, a series about building a platform on your own hardware and then arguing about whether to.

