## What the edge is made of

Four things, in order.

- **A provider load balancer**, created because a `Service` of type
  `LoadBalancer` exists. On Hetzner it terminates the TCP connection and opens
  a new one to a node — the detail that costs you the client address.
- **Traefik**, two replicas on different nodes, serving Gateway API only — no
  `Ingress`, no `IngressRoute`, no experimental channel.
- **One Gateway**, owned by the platform, in the `traefik` namespace, owning
  the addresses, the ports, the certificates, and which namespaces may attach
  anything at all.
- **cert-manager**, the only thing in the cluster that obtains a certificate.

Measured on a live cluster at rest, that whole edge is 2 pods, 4m of CPU and
150 Mi of memory — not the expensive part of the platform, the observability
stack is, but the public part, where a mistake is visible to strangers.

## Websockets: the upgrade works and nothing else does

A websocket is an ordinary HTTP/1.1 request carrying `Upgrade: websocket`,
answered with `101`; the same TCP connection then carries frames. To a reverse
proxy it is a request that never ends — no separate listener, port or route
kind. `HTTPRoute` is correct and sufficient, and ingress-nginx says the same:
*"Support for websockets is provided by NGINX out of the box. No special
configuration required."* So if a websocket never connects, the cause is TLS,
routing, or your application. If it connects and then dies, it is one of these.

**Timeouts set for a different kind of traffic.** A proxy cannot tell an idle
websocket from a hung backend, so every "this is taking too long" timer applies
to a connection *supposed* to take long. From the primary docs:

| Proxy | Setting | Default |
|---|---|---|
| nginx | `proxy_read_timeout` | 60s |
| ingress-nginx | `proxy-read-timeout`, `proxy-send-timeout` | 60s |
| Traefik | `respondingTimeouts.readTimeout` | 60s |
| Traefik | `respondingTimeouts.writeTimeout` | 0s |
| Traefik | `respondingTimeouts.idleTimeout` | 180s |

ingress-nginx is explicit about the fix — *"A more adequate value to support
websockets is a value higher than one hour (3600)."* — but raise it only on the
entrypoint carrying long-lived traffic: a raised read timeout everywhere is a
raised tolerance for a hung backend everywhere.

**Heartbeats, which are the primary defence.** RFC 6455 has Ping and Pong
frames for exactly this, and a ping every 30 seconds resets every idle timer in
the path — including a corporate proxy's, a carrier NAT's or a CDN's, which is
why heartbeats rather than raised timeouts are the defence that carries.

**Sticky routing, which is usually the wrong instinct.** One connection is one
TCP stream to one pod for its whole life; the next connection needs the same
pod only if the server keeps session state in memory, and with state in Redis
or Postgres affinity is dead weight that ruins load distribution. Each
mechanism also fails its own way: `sessionAffinity: ClientIP` behind a proxy is
one address for everyone, collapsing all users onto one backend;
`sessionPersistence` is GEP-1619, still Experimental; cookie stickiness does
nothing for native mobile or Go clients, which send no cookies.

**And the one that actually bites: a reverse proxy is a shared restart
domain.** Restarting it destroys every connection through it at once, and a
grace period is a deadline rather than a reprieve: Kubernetes gives a pod a
termination period that *"defaults to 30 seconds"*, Traefik's `graceTimeOut`
defaults to 10s, during which *"no new requests are accepted"*. A 200 ms
request finishes inside that; a six-hour session never will. A disruption
budget and node anti-affinity stop the whole edge going at once but save no
individual connection: worst case is one disconnect per replica as the rollout
walks through, then every client reconnects in the same second — so exponential
backoff **with jitter**.

Hence the rule: **do not put long-lived connection protocols behind the same
proxy as short-lived ones.** Short-lived traffic tolerates restarts, so it sets
the schedule. If sessions run to hours *and* a reconnect is user-visible, run a
second gateway deployment: same chart, separate restart domain, one more pod
per replica.

## The client address you no longer have

Behind a Hetzner load balancer, every request arrives from the load balancer —
not because of `externalTrafficPolicy` but because the balancer terminates the
connection and opens a new one, so no policy setting recovers the address.
Recovering it needs PROXY protocol, two changes made together: the annotation
on the Service, and `proxyProtocol.trustedIPs` on the entrypoint. The
annotation alone breaks every request. The entrypoint alone, or a trusted range
wider than the balancer, lets a client forge its own address.

**Rate limiting, IP allowlists and abuse investigation are not degraded when
the address is wrong — they are inverted.** A rate limiter keyed on one uniform
address either never fires or blocks everyone. An allowlist for an admin path
compares against the proxy's address and therefore allows the entire internet.

Add a CDN and the problem reappears one layer out, as headers. Cloudflare sets
`CF-Connecting-IP` and `True-Client-IP` and appends to `X-Forwarded-For`,
recommending the first two because they *"have a consistent format containing
only one IP address"*. But a header is a string the client chose: trust
`CF-Connecting-IP` from any source and anyone reaching your origin directly
sets it to whatever they like — Cloudflare says so plainly for stacked setups,
*"its value can be spoofed to any value"*. Condition trust on the source
address of the TCP connection, restricted to the CDN's published ranges.
Traefik has `forwardedHeaders.trustedIPs` for that, and also
`forwardedHeaders.insecure`, of which its own documentation says: *"We
recommend to use this option only for tests purposes, not in production."*

**Trusting from the wrong source is strictly worse than not trusting at all**,
because it converts a missing feature into a working bypass — which is why
header trust and the origin firewall are one change, not two.

## Should you put a CDN in front

By CDN I mean a *proxying* one — Cloudflare's orange cloud, Fastly, CloudFront
with a custom origin — that terminates TLS and forwards to your origin. If
yours only serves static assets from a separate hostname, almost none of this
applies.

**Probably not, if** your traffic is mostly long-lived connections, or you
handle health, legal, financial or credential data: the first adds a party with
timers you cannot read to a protocol whose whole problem is timers, the second
puts a party that sees the plaintext of every request and response inside your
compliance scope as a processor — not a risk to mitigate, the operating model.

**Maybe, if** you want caching in front of a single-region origin, or bot
management, which is genuinely hard to build.

**Yes, if** volumetric DDoS is a live threat. On a small cluster that is the
strongest argument by far: the alternative to absorbing an attack is your
provider null-routing the address, and you have no move then.

It need not be all-or-nothing — proxy the web hostname, leave the websocket
hostname unproxied, which is what Cloudflare itself recommends for work
exceeding its HTTP ceiling.

Five rows change the moment you turn it on, three of them silently.

| | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Who terminates TLS for your users | You | Them |
| What the origin sees as the client | The client | Their edge |
| How ACME validates your domain | HTTP-01 works | Use DNS-01 |
| Who can reach your origin | Anyone | Anyone, until you close it |
| Who is in the request path | You | You and them |

**Certificates move to DNS-01.** HTTP-01 is a fetch — Let's Encrypt only
allows it on port 80, follows up to 10 redirects, and it *"cannot be used to
issue wildcard certificates"* — so once the hostname resolves to the CDN,
whether that fetch reaches cert-manager's solver pod is decided in someone
else's dashboard, and a bot rule, cache rule or "Always Use HTTPS" toggle added
months later breaks it, discovered at renewal. DNS-01 never touches the request
path. Contain its credential risk with CNAME delegation: point
`_acme-challenge` at a throwaway zone, scoped to that zone alone, so a leak
buys certificates for nothing you own.

**Origin concealment is not a control.** The address leaks through historical
DNS, any unproxied record in the same zone, outbound connections your origin
makes, and Certificate Transparency — every hostname you certify is a public
record. Defend it instead with a firewall restricted to the CDN's ranges,
refreshed automatically; Hetzner's Cloud Firewalls default the right way, *"If
you do not set any rule, all inbound traffic will automatically be blocked"*.

**The firewall alone is not enough.** An allowlist of a shared CDN's ranges
permits every other customer of that CDN: anyone can sign up, point their zone
at your address and arrive from an allowed range. Cloudflare's answer is
Authenticated Origin Pulls, and the caveat is the point — global AOP proves
*Cloudflare*, not *your account*, so only per-zone AOP with your own
certificate distinguishes you, and firewall plus global AOP looks like two
independent controls and is one.

Two more costs: their outage is your outage for every hostname you proxied, and
failover is ordered — open the origin firewall **first**, then unproxy the DNS
record, or you are down between the steps, bounded by TTL rather than by how
fast you type.

What you get is real, and the free tier is genuinely useful: DDoS absorbed
before it reaches a link you pay for, TLS terminated near the user with HTTP/3
you maintain none of, a global cache, bot management. The managed rulesets
people mean by "we have a WAF" are on the paid plans.

## WAF: theirs, yours, or neither

**Theirs** runs at the edge, so blocked traffic costs you no bandwidth, CPU or
pod, and it sees real client addresses natively. Against: you cannot read the
rules, test them offline, reproduce a block locally, or version them next to
what they protect, so every "the WAF is blocking our API" incident becomes a
dashboard investigation rather than a diff.

**Yours** puts the rules in git, identical in staging and production, with a
detection mode you can read with full request context. The credible open-source
engine is OWASP Coraza, whose own maturity table is worth reading first: the
Caddy plugin and proxy-Wasm are stable, the **Traefik plugin is documented as
preview**, so for a Traefik-fronted cluster the WAF would be less mature than
the proxy it runs inside. Nor is ingress-nginx with ModSecurity a way round
that — the maintainers have announced maintenance mode, with *"1.13 in all
likelihood ... the last minor release"* and a migration expected to take about
two years.

**Neither** is usually the best value. Request size limits, per-route rate
limits, blocking obviously hostile paths and agents, and a short list of
targeted rules for the framework you run catch most of what a Core Rule Set
install catches in practice: a few dozen lines, almost no false positives,
every one understood. CRS in blocking mode without a tuning period breaks
legitimate traffic — uploads, rich-text bodies, anything with SQL-like strings
in a legitimate field — and the standard outcome is somebody disabling it
during an incident and never turning it back on, leaving you the cost, the
config and none of the protection.

One ordering constraint if you do self-host: a WAF sees the CDN's address for
every request until forwarded-header trust is correct, so client addresses
first, WAF second, or its rate limiting and IP rules are wrong from day one.

## What the kit does by default

**cert-manager is the sole ACME client, and that is what buys the second
replica.** An ingress controller with a built-in ACME resolver keeps
certificate state in a local file, so two replicas race: both notice a missing
certificate, both order it, and the account's issuance limits pay for it. The
universal workaround, pinning the ingress to one replica, makes the most
traffic-critical component in the cluster a single point of failure to solve a
problem cert-manager does not have — it issues into a Secret that any number of
replicas read.

Then hunt the effects. That single replica is why the disruption budget was
disabled in every guide that copied it; remove the cause and the budget comes
back at `minAvailable: 1`, with a *required* anti-affinity rule so the two
replicas are not both on the node about to reboot. The kit fails CI if a
certificate resolver reappears in the Traefik values, because a comment saying
"no ACME here" is not evidence of it.

**There is no WAF, and a proxying CDN is opt-in** — not because either is a bad
idea, but because turning the CDN on is four changes made together: DNS-01
issuance, forwarded-header trust, an origin firewall with per-zone AOP, and an
explicit WAF decision with a named owner. Shipping it on by default would ship
the appearance of them.

**The experimental Gateway API channel is off**, so no `TCPRoute` or `TLSRoute`
through this proxy — partly the API's own breaking-change warning, partly the
restart domain: routing raw TCP through the shared gateway looks like it solves
the long-lived-connection problem because the protocol is no longer HTTP, but
it solves multiplexing, not lifetime.

## What I would test next

The websocket and CDN sections are read from primary documentation, not
measured. In rough order:

- **Watch a `101` come back through this gateway.** Traefik has no websocket
  configuration surface, consistent with "nothing to configure", and the kit is
  written that way — but no current Traefik documentation page I could find
  states it either way, so it wants observing rather than assuming.
- **Time an idle upgraded connection.** Whether Traefik's `readTimeout`,
  documented as covering "reading the entire request", still applies after an
  upgrade is not stated anywhere I could find. Open a connection, send nothing,
  see how long it survives.
- **Measure Cloudflare's websocket idle timeout**, which is unpublished: their
  docs say only that they close a connection when no data is transmitted for a
  period, and that Enterprise customers can have a custom value. The widely
  repeated "100 seconds" figure is not in their documentation and I could not
  verify it from a primary source. The HTTP path *is* documented — a 125-second
  proxy read timeout and a 30-second write timeout, which kills long-polling
  fallbacks and slow API calls, and which a heartbeat does not help with.
- **Run the PROXY protocol path**, which the kit ships as two commented-out
  blocks with a warning above them.
- **Put a CDN in front and find out what it breaks.** None ever has, so the CDN
  sections are a checklist to verify, not a report.

## Three failures that filed no complaint

Three edge failures, in the order I met them. None crashed. All three reported
success on the way past, and each surfaced several layers from its cause.

## Lesson one: the load balancer that never existed

The Service that creates the load balancer carries two provider annotations: a
location and a type. I left one at `CHANGE-ME`.

The Helm release installed. The reconciliation reported success. The pods were
ready. The cloud controller then rejected the value with "location or network
zone not found", in a Service event nobody reads, and retried silently for
**fifty minutes** before I looked.

The result is an ingress with no address, on a cluster where every object you
would think to check is green. The location must also match where your nodes
are, and the *type* must be one currently available there: "supported" and
"available" differ in a provider API, and a merely supported type applies
cleanly and fails at creation.

**A placeholder that fails loudly is a good placeholder. This one applied.**

## Lesson two: an email address became a routing problem

The certificate issuers shipped with a placeholder contact address. Let's
Encrypt rejects that outright:

```
Failed to register ACME account: 400 urn:ietf:params:acme:error:invalidContact
```

Follow the chain. No ACME account means no certificate. No certificate means
the Secret the listener's `certificateRef` names never exists. A listener
referencing a Secret that does not exist makes the whole Gateway
`Accepted=False` — *"All Listeners must be valid"* — and a Gateway in that
state accepts no routes at all. The platform reconciliation waits for health,
so it never finished, and everything behind it never reconciled either.

An unusable email address, presenting as a routing problem four layers from its
cause, on a fresh install. The fix was not a better error message, which I do
not control; it was moving the issuers out of the default apply path, so the
cost of leaving a placeholder unedited is the feature that needs it, not
everything downstream.

## Lesson three: two permissions that sound identical

Gateway API has two cross-namespace mechanisms, both described as
"cross-namespace permission". They do different jobs.

`allowedRoutes` lives on the listener, the platform sets it, and it decides
**which namespaces may attach a route** to that listener. `ReferenceGrant`
lives in the namespace being referenced, its owner writes it, and it decides
**whether an object may reference an object in another namespace** — a route's
`backendRef` to a Service elsewhere, a listener's `certificateRef` to a Secret
elsewhere.

A `ReferenceGrant` does not let anyone attach a route. `allowedRoutes` does not
let a route reach a Service in another namespace. Assuming either covers the
other leaves one of the two open, and the failure is quiet in both directions:
a route that may attach to no listener reports the failure on *itself* and
nothing at all on the Gateway, so the Gateway looks perfectly healthy.

**The permission is granted by the party giving something up, not claimed by
the party taking it.**

## Where I land

For a small self-hosted cluster serving ordinary short-lived HTTP to a regional
audience: **no CDN, no WAF.** Spend the effort on the two things that are free
— client addresses, because everything built on top of them is inverted rather
than merely absent until you do, and the few dozen lines of size limits, rate
limits and targeted rules. Add a proxying CDN the day volumetric DDoS becomes a
real threat, in front of the short-lived traffic only, and measure it after
every ingress change.
